export default {"id":"djing-level-3","number":3,"title":"Ear Craft (Beatmatch, Cue, Phrase) · Gate A","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"Harmonic and bass clashes are not tempo problems. They are addressed with EQ and key selection later. Tempo adjustment is not used to conceal key clash. Live can synchronize warped clips to one Set tempo. Professionals use synchronization t","modules":[{"id":"djing-m-3-1","code":"3","title":"Ear Craft (Beatmatch, Cue, Phrase) · Gate A","blurb":"Harmonic and bass clashes are not tempo problems. They are addressed with EQ and key selection later. Tempo adjustment is not used to conceal key clash. Live can synchronize warped clips to one Set tempo. Professionals use synchronization t","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"djing-l-o-3","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"overview","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module 3 — Ear Craft (Beatmatch, Cue, Phrase) · Gate A","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Objective:</strong> Detect tempo and phase lock, place cues, and enter on musical boundaries with tempo sync disabled.   <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate performance gate. This module is scored.   <strong>Sources:</strong> <em>How-to-DJ</em> §2.1–2.4; Berklee Online fundamentals (drop on beat 1, beatmatching, one-ear cue, phrase mixing); ear-before-tools instructional framing."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"djing-l-3-1-1","title":"3.1.1 — Beatmatching by Ear (Tempo and Phase)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Definition of lock","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Beatmatching</strong> is shared tempo and phase so that percussion, especially kicks, forms one pulse. Failure produces flams, gallops, or doubled kicks."},{"type":"prose","html":"Harmonic and bass clashes are not tempo problems. They are addressed with EQ and key selection later. Tempo adjustment is not used to conceal key clash."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Rationale for sync-off practice","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Live can synchronize warped clips to one Set tempo. Professionals use synchronization tools. Auditory alignment develops detection of incorrect warp markers, pushed or pulled groove, and the need for phase correction under pressure. For this course: tempo sync is off for Gate A drills. Later, sync is a tool, not a substitute for correct warps (<em>How-to-DJ</em> §2.1)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure — Drill A (same track, two decks)","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load the same song on two tracks or clips.  ","Do not use waveforms as the primary guide.  ","Disable sync and master-follow aids.  ","Loop the first 4–8 bars of each.  ","Start A; start B; match tempo, then adjust phase until kicks lock.  ","First practice on speakers (clash is clear); then use cue headphones with A on the room path."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure — Drill B (different tracks, similar BPM)","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Two dance tracks within a modest BPM window (heuristic ±5–10 BPM — guideline only).  ","Match by ear first; check displays only after lock is heard.  ","Hold lock for 32 bars without visual correction."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Common errors","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Relying on waveforms while not listening  ","Matching tempo but not phase (constant kick offset)  ","Declaring lock while hi-hats remain out of phase  ","Attempting large tempo jumps before warping discipline (Module 6)  "],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 3.1.1 (scored)","blocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["Drill","Target","Self-score"],"rows":[["Same-track lock","8 of 10 trials lock within 20 seconds","/10"],["Different-track hold","Hold 32 bars clean on 5 of 5 trials","/5"],["Blind re-check","3 trials with eyes closed after initial set","/3"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Pass criteria:</strong> At least 80 percent on same-track trials; at least 4 of 5 different-track holds; written note of failure type for each miss (tempo, phase, or attention)."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"djing-l-3-2-1","title":"3.2.1 — Cueing, Drop on Beat 1, Phrase Mixing · Gate A","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.1 — Cueing, Drop on Beat 1, Phrase Mixing · Gate A","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Cue function","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"A <strong>cue</strong> is a stored start point used under time pressure. Prefer a clear downbeat, often a kick at phrase start. Avoid ambiguous intro material that delays accurate entry. Launch so that beatmatching and phrasing occur together."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Workflow:</strong> Set cue → preview on the cue bus (room remains on main) → match → wait for the master track’s phrase boundary → launch or open into the mix."},{"type":"prose","html":"Fundamentals sequences typically teach: locate beat 1 → cue and play discipline → headphone monitoring patterns → phrase mixing as distinct from raw beatmatching."},{"type":"heading","text":"Phrase-aware mix rule","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Count bars of the playing track to the next phrase start.  ","Trigger the incoming track from its phrase-ready cue on that boundary.  ","Blend over a phrase or half-phrase with EQ discipline. Do not open both full mixes simultaneously without space management."],"ordered":true},{"type":"heading","text":"Procedure — drop-on-beat-1 series","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Cue B on a clear beat 1.  ","Count A’s phrase aloud or with muted tap.  ","Fire B on beat 1 ten times in succession.  ","Score each attempt: on time, late, or early. Late entry is the common failure under pressure."],"ordered":true},{"type":"heading","text":"Procedure — phrase mix laboratory (10 pairs)","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"For each pair: cue → match → phrase entry → 8- or 16-bar blend → clean exit. Log failures with cause codes:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Code","Meaning"],"rows":[["T","Tempo unlock"],["P","Phase drift"],["F","Phrase miss"],["V","Vocal or density clash"],["G","Gain jump"]],"caption":null},{"type":"heading","text":"Gate A — self-assessment","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"All of the following must be true on a representative attempt, not only a best take:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Beatmatching, phrasing, and cueing explained as a system in 60 seconds or less  ","[ ] 8-bar phrases counted on two dance tracks without looking at the screen  ","[ ] Usable cue set on a kick downbeat on three new tracks  ","[ ] B started on A’s phrase boundary with kicks approximately aligned on at least 8 of 10 attempts  ","[ ] Recorded 5-transition mini-lab with cause codes for every miss  "],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>If criteria are not met:</strong> repeat Lesson 3.1.1 holds and the drop series for three practice days before re-testing. Do not proceed with incomplete evidence."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Module 3 checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Sync-off ear craft demonstrated under time pressure  ","[ ] Cue workflow automatic  ","[ ] Gate A checklist complete with evidence (recording and log)  "],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
